# Authentication & Authorization

## JWT

- **Access token:** Short-lived (15–60 min); in Authorization header or memory; verify with secret, issuer, audience
- **Refresh token:** Long-lived (7–30 days); HttpOnly cookie; use to issue new access token; rotate on use
- **Payload:** Include `sub` (user id), roles, and minimal claims; do not store secrets in payload
- **Verify:** Use library (e.g. jsonwebtoken); handle TokenExpiredError and JsonWebTokenError; return 401 with clear message

## Sessions (Alternative)

- Server-side session store (Redis, DB); session ID in cookie (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite); validate session on each request
- Use for stateful auth when JWT is not preferred

## Middleware

- Extract token from header or cookie; verify; attach user/roles to request; next() or 401
- **Passport:** Use strategies (JWT, local, OAuth); protect routes with authenticate middleware

## Authorization (RBAC)

- After authentication, check role/permission for route or resource; fail with 403 if forbidden
- Prefer resource-level checks (e.g. "can edit this post") over role-only where needed

## Conventions

- Secrets in env; never log tokens; HTTPS only; short-lived access tokens; validate on every protected request

## Anti-Patterns

- Storing sensitive data in JWT; no expiry; missing audience/issuer; role check without auth

